    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d129">

                <pb facs="0151" n="145" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg145"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.6111/10: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador to the Polish Government in Exile</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Biddle</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-04-27T12:00:00+02:00">April 27, 1942—12
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Polish Series 42. Supplementing my No. 41, April 27, 7 p.m. As pointed out in my
                    No. 34, April 24, 6 p.m. both Sikorski and Raczynski originally intended to
                    couch the Polish Government’s reply (Polish note of April 21) to Eden’s note of
                    April 17 in more conciliatory tone than that adopted by Sikorski in his letter
                    of April 16. However, the mood of the Polish National Council during its session
                    of April 21 was such that Sikorski and Raczynski found it necessary to take a
                    strong line.</p>
                <closer>[<signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
